1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,107
Why is the sky blue?

2
00:00:02,607 --> 00:00:06,305
And if it's blue all day, why does it turn red at sunset?

3
00:00:07,805 --> 00:00:09,925
It isn't a reflection of the sea.

4
00:00:10,425 --> 00:00:12,723
And it isn't the colour of space.

5
00:00:12,723 --> 00:00:13,768
Space is black.

6
00:00:14,268 --> 00:00:18,399
Both colours come from the same sunlight and the same air.

7
00:00:18,399 --> 00:00:23,172
The only thing that changes is how much air the light has to cross.

8
00:00:25,272 --> 00:00:26,557
Start with sunlight.

9
00:00:26,557 --> 00:00:30,025
It looks white, but it's really a mix of every colour.

10
00:00:30,525 --> 00:00:34,944
Light travels as a wave, and each colour has its own wavelength.

11
00:00:35,444 --> 00:00:40,847
Red waves are long, about 700 nanometres from one crest to the next.

12
00:00:41,347 --> 00:00:44,988
Blue waves are shorter, about 450.

13
00:00:46,488 --> 00:00:51,091
In empty space, all the colours travel together in a straight line.

14
00:00:51,091 --> 00:00:52,671
Nothing separates them.

15
00:00:54,471 --> 00:00:56,319
Then the light reaches the air.

16
00:00:56,819 --> 00:01:00,792
Air is mostly nitrogen and oxygen molecules.

17
00:01:01,292 --> 00:01:05,626
Each one is more than a thousand times smaller than a wave of light.

18
00:01:06,126 --> 00:01:11,078
When a light wave passes a molecule, it shakes the electrons inside it.

19
00:01:11,578 --> 00:01:16,253
The molecule then sends a little of that light back out, in every direction.

20
00:01:16,753 --> 00:01:18,492
This is called scattering.

21
00:01:18,492 --> 00:01:22,168
The light isn't absorbed, and it doesn't change colour.

22
00:01:22,168 --> 00:01:23,907
It only changes direction.

23
00:01:26,007 --> 00:01:27,433
But here's the catch.

24
00:01:27,433 --> 00:01:30,488
Molecules don't scatter every colour equally.

25
00:01:30,988 --> 00:01:37,061
A shorter wave shakes the electrons faster, and a faster shake sends out far more light.

26
00:01:37,561 --> 00:01:38,813
The rule is steep.

27
00:01:38,813 --> 00:01:43,613
Cut the wavelength in half, and the scattering goes up sixteen times.

28
00:01:44,113 --> 00:01:48,294
So blue light is scattered about six times more strongly than red.

29
00:01:49,794 --> 00:01:51,197
Watch what that does.

30
00:01:51,197 --> 00:01:57,607
Send 1,000 red photons and 1,000 blue ones straight down through the whole atmosphere.

31
00:01:58,107 --> 00:02:00,808
Of the red, fewer than forty are scattered.

32
00:02:00,808 --> 00:02:02,630
The rest go straight through.

33
00:02:03,130 --> 00:02:06,402
Of the blue, about two hundred are knocked sideways.

34
00:02:07,902 --> 00:02:13,391
Lord Rayleigh worked this out in 1871, so it's called Rayleigh scattering.

35
00:02:13,891 --> 00:02:16,959
At first, people thought dust was doing it.

36
00:02:16,959 --> 00:02:21,454
Later, Einstein showed that the air molecules alone are enough.

37
00:02:23,254 --> 00:02:27,590
Now stand on the ground at midday, and look up, away from the sun.

38
00:02:28,090 --> 00:02:30,395
There's no lamp in that direction.

39
00:02:30,395 --> 00:02:35,004
Without air, that patch of sky would be black, as it is on the Moon.

40
00:02:35,504 --> 00:02:38,259
But sunlight is streaming through the air above you.

41
00:02:38,759 --> 00:02:43,055
And the molecules up there are scattering a little of it down toward you.

42
00:02:43,555 --> 00:02:45,649
What they scatter is mostly blue.

43
00:02:46,149 --> 00:02:48,926
It happens in every direction you look.

44
00:02:48,926 --> 00:02:50,919
So the whole sky glows blue.

45
00:02:52,419 --> 00:02:55,207
The blue sky is sunlight that took a detour.

46
00:02:56,707 --> 00:02:59,831
Meanwhile, the sun itself still looks almost white.

47
00:03:00,331 --> 00:03:03,397
With the sun overhead, the layer of air is thin.

48
00:03:03,397 --> 00:03:07,167
About four fifths of the blue still comes straight through.

49
00:03:07,667 --> 00:03:11,204
Look near the horizon, though, and the blue turns pale.

50
00:03:11,704 --> 00:03:16,590
That light has crossed so much air that it's been scattered again and again.

51
00:03:16,590 --> 00:03:19,098
The colours end up mixed back together.

52
00:03:19,598 --> 00:03:24,251
And if you climb higher, there's less air above you to scatter light.

53
00:03:24,251 --> 00:03:27,083
The sky darkens, and in space it is black.

54
00:03:29,183 --> 00:03:29,870
But wait.

55
00:03:29,870 --> 00:03:32,922
Violet waves are even shorter than blue.

56
00:03:33,422 --> 00:03:36,717
They're scattered nearly ten times more than red.

57
00:03:36,717 --> 00:03:38,601
So why isn't the sky violet?

58
00:03:39,101 --> 00:03:40,112
Three reasons.

59
00:03:40,112 --> 00:03:44,012
The sun gives off less violet than blue to begin with.

60
00:03:44,512 --> 00:03:48,095
Some of that violet is absorbed high in the atmosphere.

61
00:03:48,595 --> 00:03:52,591
And our eyes are much less sensitive to violet than to blue.

62
00:03:53,091 --> 00:03:57,567
Put those together, and the mix that reaches your brain reads as blue.

63
00:03:59,667 --> 00:04:01,369
Now let the sun go down.

64
00:04:01,869 --> 00:04:05,185
The atmosphere is a thin shell around the Earth.

65
00:04:05,185 --> 00:04:08,709
At midday, sunlight takes the short way through it.

66
00:04:09,209 --> 00:04:13,954
At sunset, the light comes in almost flat, skimming along the ground.

67
00:04:14,454 --> 00:04:17,648
That path crosses about 38 times more air.

68
00:04:19,148 --> 00:04:25,514
And every molecule along the way is still doing the same thing: knocking blue out of the beam.

69
00:04:26,014 --> 00:04:28,076
Follow the beam as it goes.

70
00:04:28,076 --> 00:04:29,604
It starts out white.

71
00:04:30,104 --> 00:04:35,221
Then it turns yellow, then orange, as the blue and the green drain away.

72
00:04:35,721 --> 00:04:37,879
Over that distance, it adds up.

73
00:04:37,879 --> 00:04:41,846
In clean air, less than a thousandth of the blue is left.

74
00:04:42,346 --> 00:04:44,632
But about a quarter of the red survives.

75
00:04:45,132 --> 00:04:48,814
So the sun you see is what's left over: orange and red.

76
00:04:50,314 --> 00:04:54,249
That leftover light paints the clouds and the low sky around it.

77
00:04:54,749 --> 00:04:58,737
Dust and haze in the air can deepen the colour even more.

78
00:04:59,237 --> 00:05:03,550
Sunrise works the same way, with the light skimming in from the east.

79
00:05:05,350 --> 00:05:07,822
So where did the blue from your sunset go?

80
00:05:08,322 --> 00:05:09,744
It wasn't destroyed.

81
00:05:09,744 --> 00:05:11,948
Scattered light never vanishes.

82
00:05:11,948 --> 00:05:13,940
It only goes somewhere else.

83
00:05:14,440 --> 00:05:18,110
It left the beam along the way, far to the west of you.

84
00:05:18,610 --> 00:05:20,764
Out there, it's still afternoon.

85
00:05:20,764 --> 00:05:24,332
And that scattered light is somebody else's blue sky.

86
00:05:25,832 --> 00:05:30,428
The blue sky and the red sunset are two halves of the same sunlight.

87
00:05:32,228 --> 00:05:34,526
The same idea explains clouds.

88
00:05:34,526 --> 00:05:38,510
A cloud droplet is much bigger than a wave of light.

89
00:05:39,010 --> 00:05:42,497
Drops that big scatter every colour about equally.

90
00:05:42,497 --> 00:05:43,962
So clouds look white.

91
00:05:45,462 --> 00:05:48,298
And on Mars, it works the other way round.

92
00:05:48,798 --> 00:05:52,227
The air there is thin, and full of fine dust.

93
00:05:52,227 --> 00:05:55,198
That dust turns the daytime sky orange.

94
00:05:55,698 --> 00:05:59,460
But around the setting sun, the Martian sky glows blue.

95
00:06:01,560 --> 00:06:03,969
So the sky has no colour of its own.

96
00:06:04,469 --> 00:06:07,155
Air scatters blue light far more than red.

97
00:06:07,155 --> 00:06:11,246
Look away from the sun, and you see the blue that was scattered.

98
00:06:11,746 --> 00:06:15,454
Look toward a setting sun, and you see the red that wasn't.

99
00:06:16,954 --> 00:06:22,850
Next time you watch a sunset, remember: someone to the west of you is getting your blue.

100
00:06:23,350 --> 00:06:24,792
That's why it does that.
